{
  "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
  "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Ohio",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d4d84b175bc9-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Ohio argues that the \u201cbackground circumstances\u201d rule does not subject majority-group plaintiffs to a heightened evidentiary standard but rather is \u201cjust another way of asking whether the circumstances surrounding an employment decision, if otherwise unexplained, suggest that the decision was because of a protected characteristic.\u201d Brief for Respondent 10."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ashley Robertson",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d4d84b175bc9-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Ashley Robertson argued the cause for the United States as amicus curiae in support of vacatur."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d4d84b175bc9-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The question in this case is whether, to satisfy that prima facie burden, a plaintiff who is a member of a majority group must also show \u201c `background circumstances to support the suspicion that the defendant is that unusual employer who discriminates against the majority.' \u201d 87 F. 4th 822, 825 (CA6 2023) (per curiam )."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d4d84b175bc9-0000-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Title VII prohibits employers from discriminating against employees on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, or national origin."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d4d84b175bc9-0000-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The District Court granted summary judgment to the agency. 2023 WL 2539214, *12 (SD Ohio, Mar. 16, 2023)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d4d84b175bc9-0000-0003",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Accordingly, we vacate the judgment below and remand for application of the proper prima facie standard."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d4d84b175bc9-0001-0000",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We granted review to consider the validity of the \u201cbackground circumstances\u201d rule, and we reject that rule for the reasons set forth above."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d4d84b175bc9-0001-0001",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 13,
        "start": 13
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Sixth Circuit's \u201cbackground circumstances\u201d rule imposes a heightened burden on Title VII plaintiffs who belong to so-called \u201cmajority groups.\u201d"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d4d84b175bc9-0001-0002",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The rule was created by D. C. Circuit judges in Parker v. Baltimore & Ohio R. Co., 652 F. 2d 1012 (1981)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d4d84b175bc9-0001-0004",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "For too long, that rule put \u201ca deep scratch across [the] surface\u201d of Title VII."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d4d84b175bc9-0001-0006",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In other words, a plaintiff may establish a Title VII violation by proving that an employer took an employment action in part because of an unlawful motive."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d4d84b175bc9-0002-0000",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But, \u201c[n]othing in the text of [Title VII] indicates that Congress intended courts to maintain this dichotomy.\u201d"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d4d84b175bc9-0002-0001",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "\u201c[I]n any lawsuit, the plaintiff may prove his case by direct or circumstantial evidence,\u201d or some combination thereof."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1039",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d4d84b175bc99b5f4b1a74c5c6fab70031b128fb4aac82f4e7e9935684b1f7c0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d4d84b175bc9-0002-0002",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The McDonnell Douglas framework has been on the books for over 50 years now, and courts still report \u201ccontinuing confusion.\u201d"
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
